--TEST--
`implements I by $field;` synthesizes a forward per required member, reached through the class and through the interface alike, and an own method beats it — `rule:classes/delegation-by-field`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/delegation-by-field`: the compiler synthesizes, for every method the interface
// requires, a one-line forward onto the named property. It is a real method on
// the delegating class rather than a rewrite at the call site, which is what
// the interface-typed reads further down assert.
interface Greets {
    public function greet(string $who): string;
    public function shout(): void;
}

interface Counts {
    public function count(): int;
}

final class Polite implements Greets {
    public function greet(string $who): string {
        return "Hello, " . $who;
    }
    public function shout(): void {
        echo "HI\n";
    }
}

// The forward carries `rule:errors/propagation`'s error edge like any other call, so a throwing
// delegate leaves through it and is caught where the *outer* call is written.
final class Rude implements Greets {
    public function greet(string $who): string {
        throw new LogicError("no");
    }
    public function shout(): void {
        echo "GO AWAY\n";
    }
}

final class Three implements Counts {
    public function count(): int { return 3; }
}

// § 4: "two different fields may each satisfy a different interface in the
// same `implements` list."
final class Post implements Greets by $voice, Counts by $tally {
    private Greets $voice;
    private Counts $tally;

    public function constructor(Greets $voice, Counts $tally) {
        $this->voice = $voice;
        $this->tally = $tally;
    }
}

// § 4: "a class may still write its own method with the same name as a
// delegated one — that is an ordinary override, and wins over the synthesized
// forward." Only `shout` is written here, so `greet` is still forwarded.
final class Loud implements Greets by $voice {
    private Greets $voice;

    public function constructor(Greets $voice) {
        $this->voice = $voice;
    }

    public function shout(): void {
        echo "MINE\n";
    }
}

Post $p = new Post(new Polite(), new Three());
echo $p->greet("world"), "\n";
$p->shout();
echo $p->count(), "\n";

// The half a call-site rewrite could not answer: the receiver's static type is
// the interface, so the call dispatches on `Post`'s runtime method table and
// reaches the forward only because the forward is a row in it.
Greets $g = $p;
echo $g->greet("interface"), "\n";
Counts $c = $p;
echo $c->count(), "\n";

Loud $l = new Loud(new Polite());
$l->shout();
echo $l->greet("own"), "\n";

Post $bad = new Post(new Rude(), new Three());
try {
    echo $bad->greet("nobody"), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $refused) {
    echo "caught ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The agreement the shape asks for rather than a tenth line read off the
// output: the two spellings of one call must answer the same thing, so a
// forward that grew a second answer for the interface-typed receiver fails the
// count while still looking right on its own line above.
int $agreed = 0;
if ($p->greet("a") == $g->greet("a")) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if ($p->count() == $c->count()) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/2\n";
// covers: lang:classes/interfaces
--EXPECT--
Hello, world
HI
3
Hello, interface
3
MINE
Hello, own
caught no
agreed=2/2
